{"id": "sevt_01Hr2TgegQCzEiJ2Yu8TFx7k", "content": [{"text": "**Checkout p99 jumped from ~78 ms to 2,201 ms in the 14:38 minute and stayed at 3.7\u20134.8 s. The checkout deploy `de6541f` lines up with that onset, and the log confirms the mechanism.**\n\n**Deploy vs. metric timing**\n- `de6541f` (checkout, alice) deployed **14:38:10**. `p99_latency_ms` went 78 \u2192 **2,201** at 14:38 and 3,739 at 14:39.\n- `a9efec4` (inventory, carol, \"temporarily disable catalog cache\") deployed **14:39:40**. That is after the spike began, so it does not explain the onset. It is a separate change to track.\n- `error_rate` for checkout stayed at 0 until 14:46, then rose to 0.1\u20130.75%. That is downstream of the latency.\n\n**Diff `de6541f`**\n- The commit message says \"refactor for readability,\" but it replaces one batched query with a per-row query. The old code ran one `OrderItem.objects.filter(order_id__in=ids)` and grouped in memory. The new code runs `OrderItem.objects.filter(order_id=order.order_id)` inside a loop over each open order. This is the \"per-row query where there used to be a batch\" pattern from the runbook.\n\n**Log confirmation**\n- The per-row form `SELECT * FROM order_items WHERE order_id = ?` first appears at 14:38 in checkout logs. It runs 24 times in that minute, then 85\u2013180 times per minute from 14:39 onward. The batched `order_id IN (?)` form does not appear after the deploy.\n- Latency grows with request fan-out (one round trip per open order), which is why the spike is so large.\n- From 14:46, checkout logs `DB connection pool exhausted (active=50/50, waiters=12)` and `PoolTimeout` 500s on `/api/checkout/submit` and `/validate`. The extra queries hold connections longer, so the pool saturates. This is a knock-on effect of the same change.\n\n**Caveats**\n- I did not pull the `db_pool_utilization` metric. The pool evidence comes from the logs.\n- The inventory cache disable (`a9efec4`) is worth reviewing separately. It is the kind of \"temporarily\" change the runbook warns about, but the timing and diff do not tie it to the checkout spike.\n- Fix or rollback is outside this summary. Reverting `de6541f` (or restoring the batched query) is the direct remedy.\n\n**Root cause:** `de6541f` \u2014 the \"readability\" refactor replaced a batched `order_id IN (...)` item query with one query per open order, so each checkout summary issues N extra DB round trips that inflate p99 and then exhaust the DB connection pool.", "type": "text"}], "processed_at": "2026-10-14T11:58:56.309763Z", "type": "agent.message"}
